œIt is believed that The Family Evermore's Lady of the House, Annette Traville-Evermore, had had an affair with Lord Bryce Larreau-Borello. And that it was for this reason Lord William Evermore had declared war on the Town of Borello itself; the residents and inhabitants of Borello poor in every quality that Evermore stood proud to defend. Unfortunately, the assault on the neighboring town was unsuccessful, this due to both Lady Annette Traville-Evermore and Lady Jane Larreau-Borello assassinating their husbands in their sleep - So began the Legend of the Evermore Curse.
Spanning more than 300 years of both towns history, leaving nothing to the imagination, the journey of two high-powered families come together in sequenced 'Love gained, Love lost, Tragedy' before revealing the true facts of "Evermore" and "Borello". Revealing pages, parchments and actual accounts through Diaries, Journals and documented proofs, every detail of each character in the web of deceit, betrayal and unrelenting passion brings raw, unbridled and uninhibited descriptive essences; graphic in every nature for today's more mature minded reader.
From the original Mini-Novel: Evermore: The Bordello Tales, which was purposely written with 'Love gained, Love lost, Tragedy' in mind, Casanova Da Vinci captures the perfect atmosphere and lustre that will captivate each and every reader of its pages. For "Midnight Sun", however, a direct increase in vivid and unhinged erotic story telling brings together Animalistic behaviour like you have never experienced it before, taking the story line to a new milestone in The Bordello Tales.
"Breathe" brings back the 300 year feud, transporting the reader into a time and place where nothing is guaranteed to live forever. Walking the shadows, corridors and grounds of "Evermore", just two years after its construction, while causing controversy over the building as Lord Bryce Larreau-Borello is cheated out of bidding for the purchase, and, as they say: The rest is history...
Please Note: The Bordello Tales are, in some Mini-Novels, very mature and graphic in their nature of reading. If you are under the age of 18, then please do not read any of these books. They are for an Adult Audience Only, please respect this and come back soon when you reach the correct age. Thank You.
Casanova Da Vinci"ª

